The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```c
err = rtnl_configure_link(dev, ifm);
if (err < 0) {
    unregister_netdevice(dev);
}
```
Reason:
When `rtnl_configure_link` fails, the original code directly calls `unregister_netdevice(dev)`, which does not utilize the `->dellink()` method. This can leave the device in an invalid state if the new link operation was not completed properly, leading to resource leaks or inconsistent states.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
if (err < 0)
    goto out_unregister;
...
out_unregister:
    if (ops->newlink) {
        ops->dellink(dev, &list_kill);
        unregister_netdevice_many(&list_kill);
    } else {
        unregister_netdevice(dev);
    }
```
Reason:
The patch introduces a new error handling path that uses the `->dellink()` method when unregistering the device if `ops->newlink` is present. This ensures that the device is properly cleaned up and unregistered, preventing potential resource leaks and maintaining a consistent state when the `newlink` operation fails.

